# Answers file for `./setup.sh --config install.example.env`.
#
# Naming rule: `DSH_*` keys are runtime knobs written to .env; `ALLOWED_USER_IDS`
# and `GIT_*` are the runtime's list and repository access; the bare `CONTAINERS`,
# `DESIGN` and `PROVIDER_KEY` are installer-only answers. The names are historical
# and stay for compatibility.
#
# Every key below is optional; the installer falls back to a prompt, then to the
# default shown here. Only the keys in this file are accepted — a `.env` knob
# that is not listed here (DSH_TELEGRAM_ENABLED, DSH_RESOURCE_PERCENT is here, …)
# is rejected as an unknown answer, so set those in `.env` after the install.
# Copy this file, edit it, and keep the copy out of git: it carries the bot
# token and the updater token.
#
#   cp install.example.env install.local.env
#   ./setup.sh --config install.local.env --check     # see the plan
#   ./setup.sh --config install.local.env             # review each value, then apply
#   ./setup.sh --config install.local.env --yes       # unattended
#
# --config only loads answers: without --yes the installer still prompts for
# every value, using the file as the default. That is intentional (a review of
# what will be written); add --yes when there is nobody to answer.

# Rootless Podman for the agent (default) or none. The optional Penpot stack
# runs on rootful Podman and implies CONTAINERS=podman.
CONTAINERS=podman
# DESIGN=penpot        # optional Penpot stack, run on rootful Podman
# DSH_PLUGINS=setup    # setup installs the pinned plugins; ui = install/configure them from the Plugins page

# Where the deployment lives (default /srv/dsh).
# DIR=/srv/dsh

# The unprivileged user that runs the runtime.
# SERVICE_USER=dsh

# The web UI. DSH_BIND_IP must exist on the machine; 127.0.0.1 keeps it host-only.
DSH_PORT=4310
DSH_BIND_IP=127.0.0.1

# CPU/RAM share for the runtime, percent of this machine (0 = unlimited).
DSH_RESOURCE_PERCENT=30

# Model route.
DSH_PROVIDER=opencode-go
DSH_MODEL=deepseek-v4.1-flash

# The provider key. Written under the name the route reads
# (OPENCODE_GO_API_KEY for opencode-go, DEEPSEEK_API_KEY for deepseek-official).
# Left blank, the installer prints how to add it later. On a repair run a blank
# value means "keep the deployed key"; use --rotate-key (or put the new key
# here) to replace it. A key saved in the web UI's Models page outranks .env,
# so the installer reconciles that stored copy too -- see ./setup.sh doctor.
PROVIDER_KEY=

# Telegram. Blank disables the connector; then ALLOWED_USER_IDS is left alone.
DSH_TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
ALLOWED_USER_IDS=

# One bot has one budget (~20 messages/minute per group, shared by every topic).
# Extra publisher bots multiply it; each must be a member of the group.
# DSH_TELEGRAM_PUBLISHER_TOKENS=123:AAA...,456:BBB...

# Who may update, restart, roll back and change plugins. Blank = the owner of
# the bot chat (an upgraded deployment cannot lock itself out).
# DSH_ADMIN_USER_ID=

# Git: what the agent commits as, and where it may clone and push.
# GIT_AUTHOR_NAME=Vlad
# GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL=vlad@example.com
# One account can be given here (HTTPS or SSH); the interactive installer asks
# for as many as you want, and re-running it merges new ones into the manifest.
# GIT_PROVIDER=github            # github | gitlab | gitea | forgejo | self-hosted | none
# GIT_HOST=                      # only for gitea/forgejo/self-hosted
# GIT_AUTH=https                 # https | ssh
# GIT_TOKEN=                     # a PAT; stored in <dir>/secrets/git/profiles.json (0600)
# GIT_KEY=github_ed25519         # ssh only: key under secrets/git, or absolute
# GIT_KNOWN_HOSTS=known_hosts    # ssh only: known_hosts under secrets/git, or absolute
# Several accounts, unattended: point at a ready manifest instead.
# GIT_PROFILES_FILE=/root/profiles.json

# Bearer token for the updater service. Blank makes the installer generate one.
# Treat it as a root credential: it can restart the runtime.
DSH_UPDATER_TOKEN=
